en you sent me it's weird to talk  about her with her sitting here so I  have to talk to her and then um then she  sent me her mood board which was uh  filled with beautiful and very  sophisticated images that were more art  based than anything and it was like a  beautiful  um  uh visual timeline and and Narrative of  how she you know was envisioning the  story and I thought immediately that  um this was going to be a conversation  at a high level which is really  important to me and um and then we met  and it was like  um really exciting because I felt that  you know it's a different I've worked  with  um a couple actors who have directed and  it's really a a special experience for a  costume designer because they  intrinsically understand how a costume  can help not only their performance but  tell the story so there is an intimate  understanding of character development  through costume design with the director  who's an actor so  um that is um I love collaborating with  directors obviously that's like my first  thing and then a director is an actor  it's like another layer of  conversation at a deeper level a lot of  directors don't have the vernacular for  costumes or and it can be quite  intimidating so that was exciting I just  felt that you know from the first time  we met so did I answer your question I  went on a tangent but totally so um so  that was really like the impetus also I  have done a few films set in this  similar time period And I felt like this  was an opportunity  of course to uh not be so true to the  period as it's revealed as the story is  revealed and um you know there is this  um Orthodoxy to costume design sometimes  with period pieces where we can get very  stuck on what what was and the freedom  to create our own world  um With Victory and to be able to kind  of as I said plant Easter eggs for the  reveal was really really exciting to me  funds that talk about your  the conversations that you had with  Olivia about getting to the reveal like  how much  you know back and forth did you have  over Zoom  um about how you were going to reveal  what's actually happening  I mean  we just had to experiment I mean for us  when I started when I came in there was  already like a moving place but we  wanted to just Elevate as much as we we  could and for us the litmus test was the  the Durant scene so eight people around  the table like how do you create tension  and you stay on the attention and  everybody's involved it's like the other  thing is you you can figure out or or  action scenes you there there's it  becomes mechanical I mean it's fun but  that scene was always for us if this  scene works that scene that was sort of  the goal when when it came in Olivia  said that has to be the best scene of  the film so we work towards that uh  and the Revelation is that it's it's  really tricky because you want to give  something but how how long and what is  the the information you want to give  when you want to give so we have to  reorganize the thought process and just  uh create that we have to control I  think the thing with with editing is  just not just it's it's how do you  create  a pace that you the the audience is not  ahead of you you have to be you have to  guess so  if you become this this metronome if the  rhythm is just too safe then you lost  the audience but by controlling it by  giving a lot and then giving a little  not enough and also we had the element  of that there's a Sonic element and we  got to work with Skip and Paul we're  just incredible and that you fold into  the storytelling then just becomes this  I mean it's it's it's such a and you  have incredible performance such a gift  for an editor it was we had a ton of fun  and if you talk about landing on the  ending  well the ending was it was an ongoing  experiment I guess as it always is but  you know we really wanted to leave the  question open for the audience in terms  of what happened to Alice and I wanted  it to be optimistic